WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:09.042 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Africa episode seven of eight. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.702 --> 00:00:22.577 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Our longest lesson covers ten countries across southern Africa, from Angola to
Eswatini. Follow the coasts, the Zambezi neighbours and the two small
countries beside South Africa.

3
00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:27.443 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Angola and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:34.024 --> 00:00:43.869 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Angola faces the Atlantic below the Democratic Republic of the Congo and above
Namibia. Zambia lies east, while Cabinda is a separate northern coastal
exclave.

5
00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:58.260 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mbanza Kongo preserves the former capital of the kingdom of Kongo. Its remains
record an African political centre and the changes that followed contact with
Portuguese traders and Christianity.

6
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:10.605 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture an old royal capital on the Atlantic side of southern Africa. Angola
is above Namibia; remember that Cabinda is separated from its main territory.

7
00:01:11.298 --> 00:01:14.665 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Zambia and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:16.945 --> 00:01:27.649 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Zambia is landlocked east of Angola, below the Democratic Republic of the
Congo and above Zimbabwe. Malawi and Mozambique lie toward its east.

9
00:01:28.308 --> 00:01:41.567 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
At Mosi-oa-Tunya, also called Victoria Falls, the Zambezi drops into basalt
gorges and throws up clouds of spray. The falls are shared with Zimbabwe
across the river.

10
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:51.215 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture falling water on the southern border. Zambia is above Zimbabwe; their
shared waterfall helps keep the pair in the right order.

11
00:01:53.547 --> 00:02:03.787 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Malawi is the narrow inland country east of Zambia and west and north of
Mozambique. Lake Malawi follows much of its eastern side, below Tanzania.

12
00:02:04.447 --> 00:02:15.535 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lake Malawi National Park is famous for its diversity of fish, including many
species found nowhere else. These related fish provide an important window
into evolution.

13
00:02:16.191 --> 00:02:25.955 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture colourful fish along a long lake. Malawi is the slim inland country;
Mozambique curves around its southern and eastern sides.

14
00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:40.540 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mozambique stretches along the Indian Ocean below Tanzania and east of Malawi,
Zambia and Zimbabwe. South Africa and Eswatini lie toward its southern end.

15
00:02:41.201 --> 00:02:54.808 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Island of Mozambique preserves buildings shaped by African, Arab and
European connections across the Indian Ocean. Its town records a long history
of maritime exchange.

16
00:02:55.472 --> 00:03:06.049 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember a historic trading island beside the long eastern coast. Mozambique
is on the ocean; Malawi and Zambia behind it are landlocked.

17
00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:18.051 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Zimbabwe is landlocked below Zambia, west of Mozambique and above South
Africa. Botswana lies toward its southwest.

18
00:03:18.716 --> 00:03:35.051 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Great Zimbabwe preserves the remains of an important medieval trading city
associated with Shona civilisation. Its imposing stone enclosures challenge
the idea that African urban history began with European colonisation.

19
00:03:35.707 --> 00:03:46.795 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a stone city below Zambia. Zimbabwe sits between the Zambezi region
and South Africa, with Mozambique separating it from the Indian Ocean.

20
00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:51.947 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Botswana and its neighbours into view.

21
00:03:54.234 --> 00:04:04.474 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Botswana is landlocked above South Africa, east of Namibia and southwest of
Zimbabwe. A very short northern boundary reaches Zambia.

22
00:04:05.139 --> 00:04:19.814 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Okavango Delta spreads inland instead of flowing to the sea. Floodwater
arrives in the dry season, supporting wetlands and wildlife amid a much drier
surrounding region.

23
00:04:20.475 --> 00:04:32.201 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a river opening into an inland wetland. Botswana has a delta but no
coastline; Namibia lies between it and the Atlantic.

24
00:04:34.427 --> 00:04:45.259 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Namibia follows the Atlantic below Angola and west of Botswana. South Africa
lies south, while its narrow northeastern extension reaches toward the
Zambezi.

25
00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:59.177 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
In the Namib Sand Sea, coastal fog supplies water to a desert dune ecosystem.
Animals and plants survive using moisture that comes from the ocean air rather
than regular rainfall.

26
00:04:59.829 --> 00:05:09.593 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture fog drifting over dunes beside the Atlantic. Namibia is the western
coastal country; Botswana is inland to its east.

27
00:05:12.168 --> 00:05:25.728 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
South Africa occupies the continent’s southern end, facing both the Atlantic
and Indian Oceans. Namibia, Botswana and Zimbabwe lie north; Lesotho is
completely enclosed within it.

28
00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:39.766 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mapungubwe preserves the remains of a medieval kingdom near the meeting of the
Limpopo and Shashe rivers. Southern Africa had important political centres and
trading networks long before modern borders.

29
00:05:40.424 --> 00:05:49.851 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture an old kingdom at the northern edge of the southernmost country. Keep
Lesotho inside South Africa, and Eswatini beside its eastern side.

30
00:05:50.604 --> 00:05:55.294 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Lesotho and its neighbours into view.

31
00:06:00.881 --> 00:06:11.713 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lesotho is entirely surrounded by South Africa. It occupies a mountainous
inland area, with no border to any other country and no coast.

32
00:06:12.365 --> 00:06:23.708 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Maloti-Drakensberg landscape combines high mountain habitats with rock art
made by San people. The paintings preserve a cultural record within a dramatic
mountain setting.

33
00:06:24.359 --> 00:06:35.319 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a mountain island on land, enclosed by South Africa. Lesotho is the
fully surrounded country; Eswatini also borders Mozambique.

34
00:06:36.004 --> 00:06:40.009 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Eswatini and its neighbours into view.

35
00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:52.678 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Eswatini is the small country between eastern South Africa and Mozambique. It
is landlocked, even though the Indian Ocean lies relatively nearby.

36
00:06:53.327 --> 00:07:03.904 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Eswatini was previously known internationally as Swaziland. The country’s
present name makes a useful reminder to match modern naming with the same
small geographic location.

37
00:07:04.562 --> 00:07:16.033 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep the two small southern countries apart. Eswatini touches Mozambique;
Lesotho is entirely surrounded by South Africa. Neither has an ocean coast.

38
00:07:20.045 --> 00:07:37.145 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Angola and Namibia follow the Atlantic. Zambia lies above Zimbabwe; Malawi is
beside its long lake, and Mozambique follows the Indian Ocean coast. Botswana
is inland between Namibia, Zimbabwe and South Africa.

39
00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:59.878 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
South Africa occupies the southern end. Lesotho is entirely enclosed inside
it; Eswatini also touches Mozambique. Remember the royal capital, waterfall,
lake fish, maritime island, stone city, inland delta, fog dunes and highland
rock art.
